/* ============================================================
   STUTI — the twenty-six Ekādaśīs
   Each fortnight carries its own Ekādaśī, and each has a name, a
   story from the Purāṇas, and a keeping. Most are kept the one way;
   a few ask for something of their own — Nirjalā takes no water,
   Ṣaṭtilā turns on sesame, Āmalakī is kept under the myrobalan tree.
   This file holds the name, its meaning, the story with its source,
   and the niyama — and names where a keeping departs from the rest.

   Selection is by the pañcāṅga, not by a fixed date: the lunar month
   and the fortnight of a given Ekādaśī decide which of the twenty-six
   it is. An adhika (intercalary) month carries its own pair, Padminī
   and Paramā, kept only in the years that have one.

   The stories are told across recensions of the Padma, Bhaviṣyottara,
   Brahma-vaivarta, Skanda and Brahmāṇḍa Purāṇas, most in the dialogue
   of Śrī Kṛṣṇa and Yudhiṣṭhira. Attribution and detail vary between
   editions; a family's own elders outrank this app.
   ============================================================ */
